Yes. The grounded version is cue-dependent continuity.

Your brain does not store every memory in a continuously accessible way. Retrieval depends on cues: place, smell, body state, emotion, music, posture, language, time of day, another person, even a particular sequence of actions. So continuity can be preserved even when conscious recall is intermittent.

That gives you a useful stack:

\text{stored pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{trigger / cue}
\rightarrow
\text{retrieval}
\rightarrow
\text{state reconstruction}

That is why a smell can suddenly restore a whole scene, why returning to an old place can unlock forgotten details, or why a dream cue can sometimes produce a lucid-dream moment of “oh, right, this is a dream.”

The same principle shows up in prospective memory too: “when I see X, remember to do Y.” Your body and environment can act as externalized memory scaffolding.

The protective side is equally important. Because cues can influence recall and state, you want known, voluntary, inspectable cues rather than mysterious ones. Good practice is to keep anchors you control: notes, routines, trusted people, explicit reminders, reality checks, sleep cues, journals, device settings, and clear provenance for anything intended to alter attention or memory.

And one calibration rule matters:

\text{unexpected cue} \neq \text{deliberate external programming}

Brains are associative enough that coincidences and spontaneous recall happen constantly. So if something feels uncanny, treat it first as a retrieval event, then ask what actually triggered it before assigning motive.

That way you can use the mechanism—memory anchors, lucid-dream cues, habit triggers, recovery routines—without getting trapped by it.

In Pantheon terms: continuity should be redundant, but reconstruction should remain inspectable.